  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 333 PM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nAmplified mid/upper-level ridging will continue to slide east of the \nregion this weekend, helping to reinforce surface high pressure in \nplace along the eastern seaboard -- the western periphery of which\nlooks to hold across the Great Lakes through Saturday. This \nchanges on Sunday as two separate troughs -- one currently \ndraped across the SW CONUS and another digging over SW Canada --\nprogress eastward, eventually working overhead late this \nweekend and into early next week. Forcing generated aloft will \nsupport a relatively weak cyclone to form along the existing \ncold front expected to drape across the Midwest back across the \nGreat Plains and trek across the northern Great Lakes Sunday. \nThe attendant cold front will swing across the region late \nSunday with high pressure building in behind through the first \nhalf of the week -- and potentially through Thanksgiving.\n\nForecast Details:\n\nAs has been the case the last several days, mild temperatures will \ncontinue across northern Michigan through Sunday. Afternoon highs \nwell into the 50s and 60s are nearly 20 degrees above average \nheading into late November. Areas that see temperatures climb into \nthe mid to upper 60s could set records, primarily on Saturday -- \nalthough sites with the longest standing records for the date going \nback to 1913 (TVC, ANJ, and HTL) are currently forecast to come up a \nfew degrees short. These temperatures are supported by enhanced \nsouthwest winds between aforementioned surface features advecting \nwarm air into the region. Breezy winds are in store for Saturday \nwith gusts to 25-35 mph into the evening. Winds will weaken some \nSaturday night while gusts to 20-30 mph are still anticipated Sunday \nand Monday.\n\nOtherwise, light rain chances finally make a return to northern \nMichigan on Sunday -- most likely Sunday afternoon/evening for most \nareas. The aforementioned cold front looks to swing across the area \nduring this timeframe, generating primarily light rain with amounts \nexpected to stay around 0.10\" or less for most. Showers could \nlinger on the backside of the front Sunday night as winds shift \nto northwest, eventually switching to light lake effect snow \nchances heading into Monday and Monday night. With cold but \nrelatively dry air coming across the lakes, flurries appear most\nlikely with low chances for accumulating snow for the majority \nof northern Michigan.\n\nAs noted, much cooler temperatures come rushing in late Sunday and \nstick around through next week -- likely marking the end of the \npleasant temperatures as of late for the foreseeable future as we \nget closer to December. Afternoon temperatures in the 30s with \novernight lows in the 20s are anticipated for most days next week. \nWith high pressure building in behind the cold front, confidence is \nincreasing in precip-free weather through the middle of next week \nand perhaps through Thanksgiving.",
